    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's listen to their conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу, чтобы ты знала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want you to know.' A compact expression built around 'I' and 'knew'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я люблю тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I love you.' Notice the word order: 'I' comes first, and 'you' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Прекрати.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Stop it.' The key word here means 'stop it', which Vika combines with 'stop it'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Ты хочешь вешаться?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want to hang yourself?' Vika uses the casual singular 'you' with 'you', as close colleagues and friends do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Мне все равно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't care.' The key word here means 'to me', which Vika combines with 'the same'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Постой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait.' Notice the word order: 'wait' comes first, and 'wait' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не шучу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am not joking.' Max puts 'I' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'am joking'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Помоги!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Help!' In Russian, 'help' leads into 'help' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Что вы наделали, Виктория?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What have you done, Victoria?' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'Viktoriya'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Вы убили его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You killed him.' In Russian, 'you' leads into 'him' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And Vika jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Костя, помоги ему!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostya, help him!' Listen for the word meaning 'Kostya' right alongside 'help' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты реально повесился?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you really hang yourself?' Notice the word order: 'you' comes first, and 'hang oneself' finishes the sentence. Now let's hear the whole thing once more.",
